## G2. Quick start, as written
17: 1. Once: run `handoff settings claude --global`, `handoff settings codex --global`, or both, and add what it prints where it says. A session can do this for you.

$ handoff settings claude --global   (the lines before the settings themselves)
Put these settings in ~/.claude/settings.json, merged with any already there.
They set up every project at once, and do nothing in a project with no
.handoff/ or a session with no role. Sessions already running get them
when they start again.
In a project with a record, every Claude Code session takes the role you give
it: tell it, and it runs handoff role <role>. Until then it has none. It gets
the brief when it starts or is summarised, a hook listens for its work
whenever its turn ends, and it runs handoff without asking, but never decides,
nor edits a .handoff/ folder.


$ handoff settings codex --global   (the lines that are not the files' contents)
Put these two files in ~/.codex/, merged with any already there.
They set up every project at once, and do nothing in a project with no
.handoff/ or a session with no role. The next time Codex starts, it asks you
to review the new hooks: choose Trust all. Sessions already running get them
when they start again.
In a project with a record, every Codex session takes the role you give it:
tell it, and it runs handoff role <role>. Until then it has none, and handoff
on its own never shows it your decisions. It gets the brief when its first
turn starts and after a summary. When its turn would end, a hook listens for
its work and continues it with what arrives. And handoff runs outside Codex's
sandbox, so its notifications and pop-ups reach you; handoff decide is
forbidden.
~/.codex/hooks.json:
~/.codex/rules/handoff.rules:

# step 1 done here: the JSON it prints, saved as the scratch ~/.claude/settings.json
# it parses as JSON; hooks for: Notification, SessionStart, Stop

18: 2. In the project, run `handoff init`. It adds a `.handoff/` folder, which git ignores, with three roles: `implementer`, `reviewer` and `reviewer-2`.

$ handoff init
Created <scratch>/quick/.handoff. Git ignores it; nothing else in the project changed.
Roles: implementer, reviewer and reviewer-2; reviewer and reviewer-2 review.
Main branch: main.

handoff's hooks keep sessions on duty. Set them once, for every project:
  Claude Code:  set up, in ~/.claude/settings.json
  Codex:        handoff settings codex --global
For each CLI you use that isn't set up, run its command and do what it prints, or ask one of its sessions to.
Then give each session its role: tell it, such as "you review", and it runs handoff role <role>.
# exit 0
# git status --short --ignored: !! .handoff/ 
